 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are delivered through self-hosted WebSockets, providing fast updates without coupling board delivery to a realtime service. Subsecond updates are a benefit of this approach, not a stricter replacement for the 15-second freshness goal.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Change storage, delivery, and browser recovery form one revision protocol. The change store retains replayable event revisions and supports reliable handoff of committed changes for publication. Delivery owns WebSocket sessions, subscriptions, fanout, and connection capacity. Browsers track their last applied revision, reconnect after interruption, and catch up before resuming live updates. Connectivity failures remain visible rather than appearing to be an unchanged board.
 
