 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Committed changes over self-hosted WebSockets.** The system accepts connection-capacity and reconnect operations work to deliver changes quickly without recurring realtime-service fees or provider coupling. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because it adds repeated reads and several seconds of routine lag; a hosted realtime service was rejected because its lower connection burden comes with recurring fees and a provider-specific subscription model. This choice requires replayable revisions, reliable publication, managed socket sessions, and browser reconnect and catch-up behavior.
